HYBRID MAGNETO-REGOLITH SHIELDING FOR LONG-DURATION LUNAR HABITATS: A THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

ABSTRACT

This thesis presents a comprehensive theoretical framework for a hybrid active-passive radiation shielding system designed to protect long-duration lunar habitats. The system integrates **superconducting magnetic coils** (active shielding) with **in-situ lunar regolith** (passive shielding) to address the dual threat of Galactic Cosmic Rays (GCR) and Solar Energetic Particles (SEP).

The theoretical foundation rests upon: (1) **kinetic plasma theory** for leakage fraction in the unmagnetized ion regime; (2) **spherical harmonic field representation** for optimized coil arrays minimizing cusp leakage; (3) **radiation transport through composite media** validated against Geant4 simulations; and (4) **multi-objective optimization** with full sensitivity analysis and uncertainty quantification.

Critical contributions include: (i) a **revised parametric leakage model** $\eta_{\text{leak}}(B_0)$ exhibiting non-monotonic behavior with peak efficiency at 6–8 μT; (ii) **composition-dependent dose curves** showing 40% thickness variation between maria and highlands sites; (iii) a **complete optimization solution** with Pareto front, tornado sensitivity diagram, and break-even analysis; (iv) **failure mode analysis** establishing redundancy requirements; and (v) a **phased deployment architecture** scaling from 50 m to 1 km² settlements.

The analysis demonstrates that a **five-coil axisymmetric array** generating ~6 μT average field, combined with 2.8–3.5 m of regolith (site-dependent), achieves annual doses of 50–70 mSv with an ESM of ~500–1,000 tonnes. Variable-field operation reduces average cryogenic power to 1–2 kW—**60–100× lower than spacecraft analogs** (CREW HaT, SR2S). The system is shown to be **fault-tolerant** with passive-only fallback dose of 80 mSv/year, requiring **minimum 2× cryocooler redundancy** for mission safety.

## **CHAPTER I: INTRODUCTION & PROBLEM STATEMENT**

### **1.1 The Lunar Radiation Environment**

The Moon lacks both a global magnetic field and a substantial atmosphere, exposing surface habitats to two primary radiation hazards:

| **Radiation Source** | **Composition** | **Energy Range** | **Flux** | **Duty Cycle** |
|---------------------|---------------|-----------------|----------|----------------|
| **Galactic Cosmic Rays (GCR)** | 85% p, 14% α, 1% HZE | 100 MeV – >10 GeV/n | ~3 particles/cm²/s | Constant (solar cycle modulated) |
| **Solar Energetic Particles (SEP)** | >90% protons | 10 MeV – ~1 GeV | 10⁶–10¹⁰ particles/cm²/s | Sporadic (~1% during solar max) |

The **Bethe-Bloch stopping power** governs energy deposition:

$$-\frac{dE}{dx} = \frac{4\pi n z^2 e^4}{m_e v^2} \left[ \ln\left(\frac{2m_e v^2}{I}\right) - \ln(1-\beta^2) - \beta^2 \right]$$

For lunar regolith (effective $Z \approx 13$, $I \approx 130$ eV), this yields the **range-energy relation** $R(E) \propto E^{0.8}$ below few hundred MeV.

**Biological Limits:** NASA career dose limit = **1 Sv** (lifetime). For sustainable multi-year habitation, target: **20–50 mSv/year**.

