## Appendix E: Optimization Methodology and Sensitivity Analysis

### E.1 Equivalent System Mass Derivation

The Equivalent System Mass (ESM) is defined as the sum of the physical masses of the shielding components plus a mass penalty for the power system:

$$
\text{ESM} = M_{\text{coil}} + M_{\text{regolith}} + \alpha\, P_{\text{avg}}. \tag{E.1}
$$

Here:
- $M_{\text{coil}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N_c} 2\pi R_i\, \rho_c \frac{I_i}{J_e} f_s$, where $\rho_c$ is the linear mass density of the REBCO tape (kg/m per unit width), $J_e$ the engineering current density, and $f_s=2$ a structural safety factor.
- $M_{\text{regolith}} = \rho_r \times 2\pi R_h^2 x_r$ for a hemispherical dome of radius $R_h$ covered with thickness $x_r$ (the $2\pi R_h^2$ accounts for the curved surface area).
- $\alpha$ is the specific mass of the power system, taken as $100\ \text{kg/kW}$ for a Kilopower‑class fission reactor.

The average cryogenic power $P_{\text{avg}}$ is derived in Appendix F.4.

### E.2 Constraint Formulation and Lagrange Multiplier

The optimisation problem is

$$
\min_{\{I_i\},\,x_r} \text{ESM}(\{I_i\},x_r)
$$

subject to the radiation dose constraint

$$
D_{\text{total}} = D_{\text{GCR}}(x_r) + \eta_{\text{leak}}(\mathbf{B}(\{I_i\}))\, D_{\text{SEP},0}(x_r) \le D_{\text{lim}},
$$

magnetopause stand‑off $R_{mp} \ge R_h+\delta$, and structural stress limits. At the optimum, the dose constraint is active. Introducing a Lagrange multiplier $\lambda$, the necessary conditions are

$$
\nabla_{\{I_i\},x_r} \text{ESM} + \lambda\, \nabla_{\{I_i\},x_r} D_{\text{total}} = 0.
$$

Because $\eta_{\text{leak}}$ depends on the magnetic field configuration, this couples the passive and active design variables. The Pareto front presented in the thesis was obtained by a numerical grid search that explicitly respects all constraints.

### E.3 Tornado Diagram Construction and Uncertainty Quantification

The tornado diagram (Appendix G.6) was constructed by varying each input parameter individually by $\pm10\%$ from its baseline value while holding all others constant, then recomputing the ESM. Parameters considered included $\eta_0$, $D_{\text{lim}}$, $\alpha$, $B_c$, $\rho_r$, and $J_e$.

Uncertainty quantification was performed via a Monte Carlo simulation (10⁴ samples). Each parameter was drawn from a normal or uniform distribution centred on the baseline with a standard deviation reflecting its uncertainty. The resulting ESM distribution is strongly bimodal, with a low‑risk peak near $500\ \text{t}$ and a high‑risk tail extending beyond $2,000\ \text{t}$, dominated by the uncertainty in $\eta_0$. This directly quantifies the mission feasibility risk.

### E.4 Break‑Even Analysis Derivation

The break‑even field $B^*$ is defined as the minimum average field for which the hybrid system has equal or lower ESM than the pure‑passive solution. The pure‑passive ESM is

$$
\text{ESM}_{\text{pass}} = \rho_r\, 2\pi R_h^2\, x_{r,\text{pass}},
$$

where $x_{r,\text{pass}}$ satisfies $D_{\text{GCR}}(x_{r,\text{pass}}) = D_{\text{lim}}$ (the SEP is negligible behind thick regolith). For the hybrid system, the dose constraint is $D_{\text{GCR}}(x_r) + \eta_{\text{leak}}(B) D_{\text{SEP},0}(x_r) = D_{\text{lim}}$. Solving for $x_r(B)$ and substituting into (E.1) gives $\text{ESM}_{\text{hybrid}}(B)$. The equation $\text{ESM}_{\text{hybrid}}(B^*) = \text{ESM}_{\text{pass}}$ yields $B^* \approx 3.5\ \mu\text{T}$. For any $B > B^*$, the hybrid offers net mass savings.

