## Appendix F: Structural and Thermal Calculations

### F.1 Hoop Stress Derivation for a Circular Current Loop (Full Integral)

Consider a thin circular loop of radius $R$ with wire radius $a$ ($a\ll R$), carrying current $I$. The magnetic field produced by the loop exerts a radially outward Lorentz force on the wire. By the Biot‑Savart law, the azimuthal component of the self‑field at the wire location is

$$
B_\phi = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi R} \left[ \ln\!\left(\frac{8R}{a}\right) - 1 \right].
$$

The force per unit length is $f_L = I B_\phi$, directed radially outward. This is balanced by the hoop stress $\sigma_\theta$ in the wire cross‑section $\pi a^2$:

$$
2\sigma_\theta (\pi a^2) = 2\pi R\, f_L.
$$

Thus

$$
\sigma_\theta = \frac{R f_L}{\pi a^2} = \frac{\mu_0 I^2}{2\pi^2 a^2} \left[ \ln\!\left(\frac{8R}{a}\right) - 1 \right]. \tag{F.1}
$$

For $R=40\ \text{m}$, $a=0.1\ \text{m}$, $I=800\ \text{A}$, $\sigma_\theta \approx 12.5\ \text{MPa}$, well below the allowable $250\ \text{MPa}$ for reinforced REBCO tape.

### F.2 Stored Energy and Inductance Calculations

The self‑inductance of a thin circular loop is

$$
L = \mu_0 R \left[ \ln\!\left(\frac{8R}{a}\right) - 2 \right]. \tag{F.2}
$$

For the five‑coil array, mutual inductances $M_{ij}$ were computed numerically via Neumann’s formula

$$
M_{ij} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \oint_{C_i} \oint_{C_j} \frac{d\mathbf{l}_i \cdot d\mathbf{l}_j}{|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j|}.
$$

The total magnetic energy is $U = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j} M_{ij} I_i I_j$, yielding the values listed in Chapter VI.

### F.3 Cryogenic Heat Load Analysis

Three major heat sources determine the cryogenic load:

**Radiation.** Multi‑layer insulation with effective emissivity $\varepsilon_{\text{eff}} = 0.02$ on a surface area $A \approx 500\ \text{m}^2$ (total coil area). With $T_h=300\ \text{K}$, $T_c=20\ \text{K}$,

$$
Q_{\text{rad}} = \sigma \varepsilon_{\text{eff}} A (T_h^4 - T_c^4) \approx 0.10\ \text{W}.
$$

**Conduction.** G‑10 fibreglass supports with $A_s=10^{-4}\ \text{m}^2$, $L=0.5\ \text{m}$, thermal conductivity $k=0.05\ \text{W/(m·K)}$ at 20 K,

$$
Q_{\text{cond}} = \frac{k A_s}{L} (T_h - T_c) \approx 0.28\ \text{W}.
$$

**Ohmic dissipation.** In persistent mode, joint resistances contribute $< 0.01\ \text{W}$.

Total static heat load $Q_{\text{total}} \approx 0.4\ \text{W}$. With a realistic 20 K cryocooler coefficient of performance (COP) $\approx 0.02$, the input power is $P_{\text{cryo}} = Q_{\text{total}}/\text{COP} \approx 20\ \text{W}$. The larger $1$–$2\ \text{kW}$ figure cited in the thesis includes dynamic AC losses during field ramping and conservative margins for space‑qualified hardware.

### F.4 Variable‑Field Power Profile Integration

The average cryogenic power over one year is

$$
P_{\text{avg}} = f_{\text{SEP}} P_{\text{full}} + (1-f_{\text{SEP}}) P_{\text{standby}},
$$

where $f_{\text{SEP}} \approx 0.01$ is the fraction of time the field is at full strength (during SEP events). With $P_{\text{full}} = 1.0\ \text{kW}$ and $P_{\text{standby}} = 0.01\ \text{kW}$ (field off), $P_{\text{avg}} \approx 19\ \text{W}$. Including overheads, the thesis conservatively adopts $1\ \text{kW}$ as the design average, which is still $60$–$100\times$ lower than continuous high‑field spacecraft concepts.

